#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068910 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76450310-bc86-5f3b-9bb8-82206d89c627" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "dbad1aa8-5786-524e-9add-5295231dfd83" :question/math-academy-id "e-3557" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^-} f(x)$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^+} f(x)$ for the function given below.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9fd5dc473e15939a50fbb209475d580be540fb8c9a2bc8602a6a9724b08bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x) = 1 \\,\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x) = 1 \\,\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x)=1 \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50612b3c-6a02-5a91-86b3-bbd69c99604c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Equalities Involving Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "24c5fa9b-f91e-5088-9647-4923784c5917" :question/math-academy-id "e-7" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below?\n\n1. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$\n2. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$\n3. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)$\n\n![](images/1c/1c94c7d99009c46023556ce0f4f662f5a90ad315048099394016390552ade809.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nLooking at the graph, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0$. However, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\,\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, and as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0$. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=0 \\,\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\nI.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)\n$$\n\nbecomes $0=0$, which is true.\n\nII.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n\\text{DNE}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is false.\n\nIII.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n\\text{DNE}=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nwhich is false because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068911 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a14c94-c26b-5a3b-bbbb-6f122b5de9de" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at Endpoints"}] :question/id #uuid "b788cb59-f017-5ebe-9f67-f746517de260" :question/math-academy-id "e-772" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ (shown below) is defined on $x\\in [-2,2]$. Find the left-sided and right-sided limits for the function at $x=-2$ and $x=2$.\n\n![](images/96/963e4b2e7875c5f8156e3d11eee1851ec6b73fd296ccd4a269bbda139f4b895e.png)" :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that, approaching $x=-2$ from the right, the function approaches the value $y=4$. However, we cannot approach $x=-2$ from the left, because the function is not defined left of $x=-2$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,,\\quad\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{+}}f(x)=4 \\,\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the left, the function approaches the value $y=2$. However, we cannot approach $x=2$ from the right, because the function is not defined right of $x=2$. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2^{-}}f(x)=2\\,,\\quad\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** Here, $\\text{DNE}$ is simply short for \"does not exist\"."}]}
